# Little Prelude in D minor, BWV 926 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Identification: Mutopia calls this "Praeludium 2"; it is the D minor
# Praeambulum from the Clavierbuechlein for Wilhelm Friedemann Bach,
# BWV 926, No. 3 of the Nine Little Preludes (BWV 924-932) and No. 5 of
# the usual "Twelve Little Preludes" (PTNA encyclopedia and the
# Wikipedia list agree). The score is the 3/4 D minor piece of broken
# chords over a tonic pedal, 48 bars, so the BWV number is right.
#
# Character: a study in broken chords, eighths in the right hand over
# held bass notes, with one burst of sixteenths near the end and a
# hemiola close. Bold and rhythmic, harmony moving slowly underneath.
#
# Time signature: 3/4, 48 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI;
# the first note is on the downbeat of bar 1.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 112, 144 quarters, about 1:20.
# I found no timings for named recordings. Catalogue track times for
# unnamed performances: 1:01 (about 142), 1:16 (about 114) and 1:18
# (about 111); the PTNA encyclopedia gives about 1:10 (about 123) as a
# typical length. 112 follows the steadier two: the eighths ring, the
# sixteenths in bars 39-42 stay clear on the chip, and the piece keeps
# its weight rather than becoming an exercise.
#
# Structure (read from the Mutopia score and checked against the MIDI;
# the hemiola close follows PTNA, the rest is my own reading):
#   1-6    D minor broken chords in the top over a D pedal in the bass
#          (i, iv over D, the dominant over D).
#   7-8    Both hands in broken chords, D minor.
#   9-10   A falling scale over E (E7): toward A minor.
#   11-12  A minor in the bass's broken chords, then C minor over G.
#   13-14  A falling scale over D (D7): toward G minor.
#   15-20  A sequence through G minor and F, back to the dominant.
#   21-24  Dominant pedal: A in the bass in quarters, A7 broken chords.
#   25-38  A sequence of broken chords over a slower bass: B-flat (25),
#          D7 (27), G minor (29), E-flat (31), A7 (33), D minor (35),
#          the diminished seventh on C-sharp (37).
#   39-42  Sixteenth runs, the bass first (39-40), then the top (41-42).
#   43-44  The cadence as a hemiola: arpeggiated A7, a D minor chord
#          and the trilled C-sharp, in dotted-quarter and half-note
#          values that make the two bars one bar of 3/2.
#   45-47  Final descent in both hands, the eighths grouped in threes
#          (6/8 against the 3/4), over a D pedal, to a D major chord
#          (Picardy third) in bar 48.

tempo 112

voice mid 0          # mostly two voices; the inner notes of the broken
                     # chords belong to the top line, not a middle part

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-10: the opening over the D pedal
dyn 11 mp            # bars 11-20: the sequence through A minor and
                     # G minor, a softer manual
dyn 21 mf            # bars 21-24: the dominant pedal
ramp 25-38 f         # bars 25-38: the long sequence, building toward
                     # the runs (bars 39-48 stay at f)
lead 39-40 bottom    # the bass's sixteenth run
broaden 43-44 0.92   # the hemiola cadence, held back as one broad bar
                     # of 3/2; then a tempo for the closing descent

rit 2 0.75           # into the final D major chord
